\section*{Poisson-Dirichlet factorization of prime predecessors}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 011; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that the logarithmically normalized ordered prime factors of $p-1$, for uniformly sampled primes, converge jointly to the parameter-one Poisson--Dirichlet partition.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
For each prime $p\ge3$, order prime factors $q_j(p)$ of $p-1$ decreasingly with multiplicity, pad by ones, and set $V_j(p)=\log q_j(p)/\log(p-1)$. Take independent uniform $U_j\in(0,1)$, form stick fragments $B_j=(\prod_{i<j}U_i)(1-U_j)$, and let $(L_j)$ be their decreasing rearrangement, the Poisson--Dirichlet law with parameter one. For every fixed $k\ge1$ and bounded continuous $F:[0,1]^k\to\mathbb R$, the claimed ordinary prime-average limit is
\[
 \lim_{x\to\infty}\frac1{\pi(x)-1}\sum_{3\le p\le x}F(V_1(p),\ldots,V_k(p))
 =\mathbb EF(L_1,\ldots,L_k).
\]
The sum ranges over primes with equal weights, and the limit is through all real $x$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 011; 2026-09-24), marked divisor moments are expressed using a determinant expansion and paired endpoint weights. Independent residue lines and a symmetric memory space control the signed graph error, allowing the finite-dimensional factor statistics to match the ordinary-integer model.

